    "content": "Getting into unnecessary controversies. Re-invent yourself While the influencer boom has been materializing over the last few years, one of the big issues seems to be over their content getting stale. One of India leading influencers is increasingly facing flak on social media after peddling the same anecdotes as part of his content on everyday motivation. His content is typically on the lines of, How I failed in my 20s , How I failed in my first job , What did I do when I was broke , How did I fail my first internship . While reflecting on individual stories and motivating an audience is important, a failure in generating new engaging content signals long term decline. Going forward, like in the world of movies, only the creators who re-invent regularly can survive the distance. Can the prominent influencers re-invent? The monetisation debate every content creator frets over the Big M question how we make money out of our social networks. Over the last few years, many creators are monetising their influence. According to a report by Adobe, nearly half of non-professional creators are making money, although small sums. However, nearly 90 percent of earnings are made by the top 10 percent of influencers. The traditional routes are heavily dependent on advertising revenue, brand partnerships and a bit from your own courses. The future of creator led economy will see an increasing contribution of the subscription model. Instagram and YouTube are introducing these features globally where you have to pay to subscribe to your favorite influencer content. Similarly, the rise of platforms like Sub stack, where you pay to read the articles of your favorite influencers, suggests a tangible move in that direction. The scale-up of Web 3.0 might be a pivotal moment in that direction as the central gatekeeper (e.g., Instagram) might not be necessary for a creator to monetise as the underlying network will facilitate the transaction between the influencer and the end user. Greater role of virtual influencers everyone is talking about Catgut, a conversational AI, as it crosses 1 million users in nearly a week globally. Catgut can write essays, write apology letters, and compose poetry and songs. Given its quality of writing is impressive at first glance, it is not surprising to predict that conversational AI is likely to become the big type of influencer going forward. You should definitely give Catgut a try. The movement towards virtual led influencers is not new as virtual influencers generated $ 16 bank of commerce alone in the year 2022. In conclusion, the future of the creator economy looks bright. However, there are cracks that seem to be appearing everyday as many other stakeholders get involved. Most people believe the influencer is not a passing fad and will stay on for some time. This is likely to be true. How the influencers themselves evolve will determine that journey. I can assure you this piece has been written by a human being, Sandeep Das, and not by Catgut.",
